u1::a A promise type ::?tc
t2::a "A system outcome, usually a resource configuration, but maybe a message or behaviour." ::t
u3::a "CFEngine employs autonomous agents whose function is to keep certain \"promises\"." ::?etc
u4::a CFEngine is used for automating the configuration and maintenance of computer systems. ::?etc
u5::a CFEngine promise type ::?tc
u6::a CFEngine promises ::?tc "Some might call promises \"rules\", but they are only self-imposed rules on the agents. · The agents can only promise its own intent to maintain state. If the operating sytem blocks an agent, or some other process interferes then even though it can keep its own promise to try and repair a deviant state, it can't promise that the system will end up according to specification." ::tip
u7::a CFEngine syntax ::?tc
u8::a "Declarations of \"intended outcome\" or \"desired state\"." ::?tc
u9::a "For example, the files type promise centers around managing file presence, content, and attributes while vars type promises hold data." ::?etc
t10::a How autonomous entities called agents can declare their intended behaviour. ::t
u11::a Promise Theory ::?tc "Promise Theory has nothing a priori to do with \"promises\" in programming languages like Javascript, though programming promise functionality is compatible with the Promise Theory definition.." ::tip
u12::a "Some might call promises \"rules\", but they are only self-imposed rules on the agents." ::?tc
u13::a Suppose you have 1000 machines of different kinds ... ::?etc
t14::a The desired state and/or maintainence schedule of an affected system resource / object ::t
u15::a "There was this one time, at LinkedIn camp...." ::?etc
u16::a Use basic promises rather than embedding shell commands ::?etc
